 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** $O(N)$, where $N$ is the number of ray march steps [G].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(1)$ auxiliary memory [G].
-*   **Performance:** Adds minimal overhead compared to hard shadows, as it reuses the same ray march loop with an additional ratio accumulation [S5, S9, unofficial]. Performance scales with scene density; denser geometry may require more steps or earlier termination [G].
-*   **Strengths:** No separate pass required; works with any SDF; naturally anti-aliased; supports dynamic geometry without update costs [S8, S9, unofficial].
-*   **Limitations:** Approximate; does not simulate complex light transport (e.g., multiple bounces); quality depends on step size and $k$ parameter tuning [S6, S8, unofficial].
+*   **Time Complexity:** $O(N)$, where $N$ is the number of ray march steps. Typically fixed between 32 and 64 steps per shadow ray [S6, S7, unofficial].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(1)$. No additional memory buffers are required; it uses the same SDF queries as the primary ray march [S19, unofficial].
+*   **Performance:** Soft shadows are "almost free" compared to hard shadows in SDF ray marching because they require no additional ray casts or texture lookups, only a few extra arithmetic operations per step [S9, S10, unofficial].
+*   **Strengths:**
+    *   Produces smooth, alias-free penumbrae [S13, unofficial].
+    *   Works with dynamic geometry without requiring shadow map updates [S12].
+    *   Integrates seamlessly with existing SDF ray marching pipelines [S19, unofficial].
+*   **Limitations:**
+    *   Approximates area lights; the softness is controlled by a single scalar $k$ rather than a true light radius, which may not match physical accuracy for all light sizes [S11, unofficial].
+    *   Performance is linear with the number of lights; multiple lights require multiple shadow ray marches [S11, unofficial].
+    *   Can suffer from artifacts if the light is very close to the surface due to the linear ratio assumption [S11, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-The technique was popularized by Inigo Quilez, who demonstrated that SDFs provide global scene information useful for soft shadows without additional data structures [S8, unofficial]. It is a standard feature in raymarched SDF renderers, often cited alongside ambient occlusion as a "free" shading effect [S9, unofficial]. Variations include logarithmic adjustments for near-light stability [S6, unofficial] and scale-based penumbra control [S8, unofficial].
+The technique was popularized by Inigo Quilez, who described it as a way to obtain soft shadows with a penumbra region using distance fields by inspecting the distance $d$ from the ray to surrounding geometry and the traversal distance $t$ [S13, unofficial]. It is often referred to as "Quilez soft shadows" or "MIP shadow" in the demoscene and indie graphics communities [S1, S2, unofficial]. The method leverages the global geometric information provided by SDFs to approximate the visibility of an area light without the overhead of shadow maps or multiple ray samples [S12][S19, unofficial].
 
